* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Sep 26 Windows port - the case block takes the LINUX PORT'S SHAPE: the
*           symbol arm names 32-126 itself and the catch-all returns @false,
*           so an unmatched byte is refused whatever the arm order.  Mine put
*           the out-of-range test first and let the catch-all mean "symbol",
*           which fails OPEN if the arms are ever reordered.  Their offer,
*           19 Sep 14:00, and the behaviour is identical on all 13 rows.
* 19 Sep 26 Windows port - new.  RELEASE_1.1 75.  Owner's ruling, taken on the
*           SD Core for Linux side and binding in both ports under CLAUDE.md's
*           parity rule: "The requirement for the complex password is
*           reasonable for access to the database, if possible I would prefer
*           that SD require it even if the OS does not for both SDSYS and any
*           other accounts."
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !pw_complex(pw)
*
*    Returns @true when pw satisfies SD's password rule, @false otherwise.
*    It sets no status and touches nothing: it is a predicate over a string.
*
*    THE RULE, and it is a CONTRACT SHARED WITH THE LINUX PORT rather than a
*    local choice - the two implementations are checked against the same
*    table, so do not "improve" it on one side only:
*
*      - at least 8 characters, no maximum;
*      - at least one lower-case letter    a-z;
*      - at least one upper-case letter    A-Z;
*      - at least one digit                0-9;
*      - at least one SYMBOL, which means any OTHER printable ASCII
*        character, 32 to 126 - AND SPACE COUNTS AS ONE;
*      - any character outside 32-126 fails outright: a tab, a control
*        character, or anything non-ASCII such as an accented letter.
*
*    WHY ASCII CODE RANGES RATHER THAN CHARACTER CLASSES.  The rule is
*    written in BASIC here and in bash on the Linux side, and a byte range
*    reads the same in both; "is this a letter" does not, once a locale is
*    involved.  The Linux agent asked for it in these terms.
*
*    WHY NON-ASCII FAILS RATHER THAN COUNTING AS A SYMBOL.  A password is
*    typed again later, on a console whose encoding nobody has promised - and
*    on this port it may also be handed to Windows, which stores it as UTF-16
*    while SD holds bytes.  A character that survives one round trip and not
*    the next is a lockout, so it is refused at the door where the refusal
*    can still be explained.
*
*    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.  It does not check a dictionary, a history, or the
*    account name - Linux's PAM stack does some of that and this deliberately
*    does not try to reproduce it.  ***AND IT IS NOT THE ONLY RULE IN FORCE
*    ON THIS PORT***: the callers that set a WINDOWS password still hand it
*    to Windows afterwards, and the machine's own policy can refuse what this
*    accepts.  SD's rule is a floor, never a ceiling.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function pw_complex(pw)
$catalog !pw_complex

   pw.len = len(pw)
   if pw.len < 8 then return @false

   got.lower = @false
   got.upper = @false
   got.digit = @false
   got.symbol = @false

*  ONE PASS, AND THE CATCH-ALL FAILS CLOSED.  ***THIS IS THE LINUX PORT'S
*  SHAPE AND IT REPLACED A WORSE ONE OF MINE ON 19 Sep 2026.***  The first
*  version put the out-of-range test in the FIRST arm and let the catch-all
*  mean "symbol", which is correct only while that arm stays first: move it
*  and a byte outside 32-126 falls through to the catch-all and SATISFIES the
*  requirement it is supposed to fail - it fails OPEN, and the only thing
*  standing between the tree and that was a guard asserting nobody had
*  reordered the arms.
*
*  HERE THE SYMBOL ARM NAMES ITS OWN RANGE and the catch-all RETURNS FALSE,
*  so a character that matches nothing is refused no matter how the arms are
*  ordered.  A reordering can still break the rule - put the symbol arm first
*  and letters stop counting as letters - but it then refuses valid passwords
*  rather than accepting invalid ones, and that is the direction to fail in.
*  ***A SHAPE THAT CANNOT FAIL OPEN BEATS A CHECK THAT REPORTS IT.***
   for pw.i = 1 to pw.len
      pw.c = seq(pw[pw.i, 1])
      begin case
      case pw.c >= 97 and pw.c <= 122
         got.lower = @true
      case pw.c >= 65 and pw.c <= 90
         got.upper = @true
      case pw.c >= 48 and pw.c <= 57
         got.digit = @true
      case pw.c >= 32 and pw.c <= 126
         got.symbol = @true
      case 1
         return @false
      end case
   next pw.i

   if not(got.lower)  then return @false
   if not(got.upper)  then return @false
   if not(got.digit)  then return @false
   if not(got.symbol) then return @false

return @true
end
